# The golden-vector corpus is compared byte for byte, so git must hand
# it back exactly as committed.
#
# Git for Windows checks out text files with CRLF by default, which
# rewrites manifest.json on checkout and makes
# `tools/gen_golden_vectors.py --check` fail on Windows and nowhere
# else. The .npy files escaped because git detects them as binary and
# leaves them alone -- so the symptom was the manifest alone differing,
# which reads like a content problem rather than a checkout one.
#
# -text disables end-of-line conversion in both directions.
native/tests/golden/** -text

# Belt and braces: never let any .npy be treated as text. Git's binary
# detection is a heuristic, and a corpus file it guessed wrong about
# would be corrupted rather than merely reformatted.
*.npy binary

# Generated, and compared against a fresh generation in CI for an exact
# match. Same reasoning as the manifest.
native/core/config.hpp -text
